+      # Copy the signed digest to the secondary registry (R12-RES-9177), and
+      # read it back.
+      #
+      # A loss of the build registry's region must not lose the release
+      # artifact: the nodes keep what they pulled, but every rollback and
+      # every later promotion names a digest that lived only there. So once
+      # the digest is signed, the same bytes go to the replica the build
+      # environment's tfvars declare. Only when one is declared — the `if`
+      # is the switch, and it is off in every environment today.
+      #
+      # The digest is the one the sign step attested: both resolve the same
+      # `binary:<sha>` tag, which the registry's immutable tags pin to one
+      # set of bytes. `imagetools create` with one source and nothing to
+      # merge or annotate pushes the source's manifest bytes unchanged under
+      # the new tag and copies each blob by digest, so the copy has the same
+      # digest; a `docker pull`/`tag`/`push` goes through the daemon's store
+      # and is not guaranteed to. Binary Authorization's attestation names
+      # that digest, so it covers the copy without a second signature.
+      #
+      # Nothing is assumed to have landed. The secondary is asked for
+      # `binary@<digest>` afterwards and the run fails unless it answers
+      # with exactly that digest: a copy that failed quietly, or a tag in
+      # the replica already holding other bytes, would otherwise leave a
+      # region loss to discover that the artifact was never there.
